  "title": "Relation with earlier work.",
  "body": " Relation with earlier work   It is difficult to determine precisely how an AI system arrived at such a construction, and hence to identify every human source that may have influenced it. We therefore record the closest related work which may have contributed ideas to the constructions below.  The closest precedents for the pathology are the examples of Buzzard Verberkmoes and Mihara. Buzzard Verberkmoes construct a uniform affinoid ring with a nonuniform rational localization and, separately, a uniform affinoid ring whose structure presheaf is not a sheaf . Mihara gives further examples of uniform Banach and affinoid rings for which rational localization loses uniformity, including a separate non-sheafy uniform affinoid ring . Thus the phenomena uniform but not stably uniform and uniform but not sheafy were already known. What those examples did not supply was a proof that a non-stably-uniform example is nevertheless sheafy.  The pullback terminology also has a direct precedent. A cartesian square of rings with a surjective leg is a classical Milnor square, named for Milnor's theorem which patches projective modules from the two comparison rings . Kerz Saito Tamme already work explicitly with Milnor squares of complete Tate rings, construct compatible rings of definition by an open-mapping argument, and prove analytic -theory excision for them . Thus neither the term Milnor square , its complete-Tate version, nor the basic topological pullback mechanism is new here. We use the adjective strict to keep the topological exactness and bounded-denominator estimates visible; we have not found it used as standard terminology for this particular nonarchimedean situation. The input particular to the present application is the observation that strict Koszul regularity makes every rational localisation of the finite-jet square strict and natural under refinement, followed by the sheaf-transfer argument.  After applying the contravariant functor , the ring pullback produces a pushout-shaped diagram of affinoid adic spaces; see . It is also analogous to a nonarchimedean pinching, but Temkin's pinching theorem concerns a finite gluing morphism, whereas in the present diagram is a rational open immersion and is not finite . Finally, the graph complex used below is the strict Koszul complex of Ben-Bassat Kremnizer and Bambozzi Kremnizer . In particular, the strict Koszul regularity asserted in is not new. We retain a proof because we need the resulting lattice exponents explicitly. The finite-jet combination producing a uniform sheafy domain which is not stably uniform appears to be new.  The weighted-parity construction of is even closer in spirit to the infinite monomial-support examples of Buzzard Verberkmoes and Mihara.   "
