  "title": "Small perturbation lemma.",
  "body": " Small perturbation lemma   Let be a complete Tate Huber pair and choose a closed ring of definition . Let be a rational datum whose entries lie in . Write and for . Suppose that for some there are with Let satisfy and write . Then is a rational datum, and define the same rational subset of , and their completed rational localizations are canonically isomorphic.    Write with . Then The element is a unit of , with inverse given by its convergent geometric series. Multiplying the by this inverse gives an integral Bezout relation for the primed datum; in particular, is open.  Let lie in the rational subset defined by . Since , relation gives The perturbations have value at most , so and . This proves one inclusion of rational subsets. Applying the same argument to the primed Bezout relation proves the reverse inclusion.  Let be the first rational localization and put Then The second summand is topologically nilpotent. After adjoining the finitely many power-bounded elements involved to a ring of definition, the geometric series for its inverse and all powers of that inverse lie in the same bounded subring. Thus is a -unit with power-bounded inverse, and is power-bounded. The universal property gives a homomorphism . Repeating the argument with the primed Bezout relation gives a homomorphism in the opposite direction. Both composites restrict to the identity on ; universal-property uniqueness makes them inverse. The isomorphism is therefore canonical.   "
